# Target C-II — shallow-tooth reflection blockers and normal-aligned diameter bracing

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Repository base:** `6b9afd0870cb6148e54762088f7e70f46767a70a`  
**Status:** proved proof-only source-level reduction; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** Type-II shallow two-edge teeth in the canonical contact-minimal representative  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

The current low-`t` tooth branch already forces a linear family of pairwise distinct
shallow tooth apices. The missing point is that the resulting penetration contacts
have no uniform KKT-weight floor. This note extracts a different, weight-free
consequence from each shallow tooth by reflecting its apex across the supporting line
of its loaded base.

The reflection gives an exact source-level alternative. Either the move is blocked
from above by a source point almost at diameter from the apex and lying in a narrow
cone about the inward support normal, or it is blocked from below by an actual
contact-isolated source point outside the force hull. Contact minimality supplies the
same exterior-isolate witness if the reflected move is otherwise feasible.

A circular packing argument bounds how often one exterior isolate can witness distinct
shallow apices. At the current low-`t` tooth floor this gives the linear alternative

```text
at least 0.0423993163 D normal-aligned near-diameter shallow apices
or
at least 0.0423993163 D distinct exterior contact-isolated source points.
```

This does not close C-II, but it converts the shallow-tooth population into two
full-source obstruction families rather than an unweighted contact count.

## 1. One shallow tooth and its reflected apex

Let `a-x-b` be a two-edge outer tooth. Put coordinates

\[
a=(-c,0),\qquad b=(c,0),\qquad x=(0,h),
\tag{RB.1}
\]

where

\[
c=\cos\alpha,\qquad h=\sin\alpha,\qquad c^2+h^2=1,
\tag{RB.2}
\]

and the loaded force hull `F` lies in the half-plane `y>=0`. The loaded base length
is `2c`. For an `eta`-shallow tooth,

\[
2c\ge2-\eta,
\qquad
0<\alpha\le\alpha_\eta:=\arccos(1-\eta/2).
\tag{RB.3}
\]

Reflect the apex across its loaded base line:

\[
x^-:=(0,-h).
\tag{RB.4}
\]

For every point `y=(p,q)` one has the exact identity

\[
\boxed{
|y-x^-|^2-|y-x|^2=4hq.
}
\tag{RB.5}
\]

Hence reflection cannot decrease distance to any source point in the closed inward
half-plane `q>=0`. In particular it preserves the two contacts to `a,b` and moves
strictly away from every other old contact neighbor of `x`: every actual contact
neighbor lies in `F`, and a contact neighbor on `y=0` must be `a` or `b`.

Canonical C26 gives `deg_C(x)>=3` for the unloaded positive-contact apex, so at least
one old unit contact is strictly broken by the reflection.

## 2. Reflection-blocker dichotomy

Call a source point `z` a **lower blocker** if

\[
|z-x^-|<1,
\tag{RB.6}
\]

and call a source point `y` an **upper blocker** if

\[
|y-x^-|>D.
\tag{RB.7}
\]

Every lower blocker has negative signed height. Indeed (RB.5), together with the
original source separation `|z-x|>=1`, rules out `q>=0`. Therefore `z` lies outside
`F`. Canonical Section 56 says every actual source point outside `F` is
contact-isolated in the contact-minimal representative.

Suppose now that there is no lower blocker and no upper blocker. Replacing `x` by
`x^-` then gives another unit-separated `n`-point configuration of diameter at most
`D`. The two tooth contacts survive, while all other old contacts of `x` are broken.
If the new configuration had fewer actual unit contacts, global diameter minimality
(if the diameter dropped) or the canonical contact-minimal tie-break (if it stayed
`D`) would be contradicted. Consequently the reflected apex must acquire at least
one new unit contact.

By (RB.5), a new contact cannot come from `q>=0`; hence every new contact is an actual
source point outside `F`, and therefore is contact-isolated in the original source.
We obtain the finite alternative

\[
\boxed{
\text{every shallow tooth has either an upper blocker, or an exterior-isolate
reflection witness.}
}
\tag{RB.8}
\]

The second branch includes both a strict lower blocker and an exact new unit contact
created by an otherwise feasible reflection.

## 3. Quantitative depth of an exterior-isolate witness

Assume throughout this section that

\[
\alpha<\pi/12.
\tag{RB.9}
\]

Define

\[
Y_-(\alpha):=\sin(\pi/3-\alpha),
\qquad
Y_+(\alpha):=\sin(\pi/3+\alpha).
\tag{RB.10}
\]

### Lemma RB.1 — strict lower blockers have depth `>Y_-(alpha)`

Let a lower blocker be

\[
z=(u,-r),\qquad r>0.
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{r>Y_-(\alpha).}
\tag{RB.11}
\]

### Proof

Because `z` lies outside `F`, it is contact-isolated. Thus the original source has
strict separations

\[
|z-x|>1,\qquad |z-a|>1,\qquad |z-b|>1.
\tag{RB.12}
\]

Put `U=|u|`. If `r>=1`, (RB.11) is immediate. Assume `r<1` and put

\[
B:=\sqrt{1-r^2}.
\]

Separation from the nearer base endpoint gives

\[
|U-c|>B.
\tag{RB.13}
\]

The branch `U>c+B` is incompatible with the lower-blocker inequality
`U^2+(r-h)^2<1`, because

\[
(c+B)^2-\bigl(1-(r-h)^2\bigr)
=1+2cB-2rh
>1-2h>0.
\tag{RB.14}
\]

Hence

\[
U<c-B.
\tag{RB.15}
\]

In particular `c>B`, so `r>h`. Write

\[
r=\sin\beta,
\qquad
\beta\in(\alpha,\pi/2).
\]

If `r+h>=1`, then

\[
r\ge1-h>\sin(\pi/3-\alpha),
\]

because

\[
1-h-\sin(\pi/3-\alpha)
=1-\cos(\alpha-\pi/6)>0.
\]

It remains to consider `r+h<1`. The old separation `|z-x|>1` and (RB.15) give

\[
\sqrt{1-(r+h)^2}
<U
<c-\sqrt{1-r^2}.
\tag{RB.16}
\]

Squaring the outer inequality in (RB.16), substituting `r=sin(beta)`, and cancelling
common terms gives

\[
2\cos(\alpha+\beta)<1.
\]

Since `0<alpha+beta<7pi/12`, this implies

\[
\beta>\pi/3-\alpha,
\]

which proves (RB.11).

### Lemma RB.2 — an exact new contact is even deeper

If an otherwise feasible reflection acquires a new unit contact `z=(u,-r)`, then

\[
\boxed{r>Y_+(\alpha).}
\tag{RB.17}
\]

Indeed `a,b,z` all lie on the unit circle centered at `x^-`. Separation
`|z-a|,|z-b|>1` places the direction of `z-x^-` outside the open `pi/3` neighborhoods
of the directions `alpha` and `pi-alpha`. The only two remaining arcs are

\[
(\pi/3+\alpha,\,2\pi/3-\alpha)
\]

and

\[
(4\pi/3-\alpha,\,5\pi/3+\alpha).
\]

The first arc is impossible because `|z-x|>1` is equivalent to
`sin(theta)<h`, while every angle in that upper arc has
`sin(theta)>sin(pi/3+alpha)>h`. Thus `theta` lies in the lower arc. There

\[
r=h-\sin\theta
>h+\sin(\pi/3-\alpha)
=\sin(\pi/3+\alpha),
\]

proving (RB.17).

Thus every exterior-isolate witness lies a fixed positive distance beyond the support
line of the loaded base. A strict lower blocker has depth `>Y_-`; a contact created
by a feasible reflection has the stronger depth `>Y_+`.

## 4. Upper blockers are normal-aligned near-diameter points

Let an upper blocker be

\[
y=(p,q).
\]

The reflected violation and the original diameter constraints to the two loaded base
endpoints give

\[
p^2+(q+h)^2>D^2,
\tag{RB.18}
\]

while

\[
(p+c)^2+q^2\le D^2,
\qquad
(p-c)^2+q^2\le D^2.
\tag{RB.19}
\]

Comparing (RB.18) with the larger of the two quantities in (RB.19) yields

\[
\boxed{
2hq-2c|p|>c^2-h^2=\cos(2\alpha).
}
\tag{RB.20}
\]

Because `alpha<pi/12`, the right side is positive. Consequently

\[
\boxed{
q>0,
\qquad
{|p|\over q}< {h\over c}=\tan\alpha.
}
\tag{RB.21}
\]

So every upper blocker lies in the inward normal cone of the loaded base, with
half-angle strictly less than `alpha`. It is also almost a diameter partner of the
old apex:

\[
\boxed{
|x-y|>D-2h.
}
\tag{RB.22}
\]

Equation (RB.22) is just the triangle inequality using `|x-x^-|=2h`, but the cone
constraint (RB.20)-(RB.21) is additional information supplied by the two loaded base
endpoints. It is the promised coupling between the shallow-tooth support normal and
the diameter scale.

No claim is made that an upper blocker lies in `F`; (RB.20) only puts it in the inward
half-plane of this particular support line.

## 5. Angular packing bounds reflection-witness reuse

Fix `eta` and put

\[
h_\eta:=\sin\alpha_\eta,
\qquad
R_\eta:=1+2h_\eta.
\tag{RB.23}
\]

If an exterior isolate `z` is a reflection witness for an `eta`-shallow apex `x`,
then either `|z-x^-|<1` or `|z-x^-|=1`. Since
`|x-x^-|=2h<=2h_eta` and `z` is contact-isolated in the original source,

\[
\boxed{1<|z-x|\le R_\eta.}
\tag{RB.24}
\]

Suppose the same `z` witnesses distinct shallow apices `x_1,...,x_M`. Write

\[
r_j:=|x_j-z|\in(1,R_\eta]
\]

and let `theta` be the smaller angular separation at `z` between two of the apex
rays, of radii `r,s`. Unit separation of the source gives

\[
1\le r^2+s^2-2rs\cos\theta,
\]

hence

\[
\cos\theta
\le f(r,s):={r^2+s^2-1\over2rs}.
\tag{RB.25}
\]

For fixed `s`, the derivative of `f(r,s)` with respect to `r` has the sign of
`r^2-s^2+1`, so there is no interior maximum in `r`. Applying the same observation in
`s` shows that the maximum on `[1,R_eta]^2` is attained at a corner. Therefore

\[
f(r,s)\le
C_\eta:=\max\left\{{R_\eta\over2},
1-{1\over2R_\eta^2}\right\}.
\tag{RB.26}
\]

Put

\[
\Theta_\eta:=\arccos C_\eta.
\tag{RB.27}
\]

Every two witness-associated apex rays have circular separation at least
`Theta_eta`. Ordering the `M` rays around `z`, the consecutive angular gaps sum to
`2pi`, so

\[
\boxed{
M\le\left\lfloor{2\pi\over\Theta_\eta}\right\rfloor.
}
\tag{RB.28}
\]

This is stronger than the enclosing-disk area bound because it uses the annular
location `1<|z-x|<=R_eta` directly.

## 6. Current low-`t` tooth branch: a stronger linear blocker/isolate alternative

Use the exact convenient value

\[
\eta={1\over19}.
\tag{RB.29}
\]

Then

\[
c_\eta={37\over38},
\qquad
h_\eta={5\sqrt3\over38},
\qquad
\alpha_\eta=0.229921841001413\ldots<\pi/12.
\tag{RB.30}
\]

At the current low-`t` floor, the tooth alternative and the metric-detour bound are

\[
{N_2\over D}\ge0.4363893718701161781\ldots-o(1),
\qquad
{d_{out}\over D}\le0.002883975+o(1).
\tag{RB.31}
\]

Hence

\[
{N_{sh}(1/19)\over D}
\ge
0.4363893718701161781-19(0.002883975)-o(1),
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
{N_{sh}(1/19)\over D}
\ge0.3815938468701161781\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{RB.32}
\]

The shallow-apex injectivity theorem makes all these shallow apices distinct.

For `eta=1/19`,

\[
R_\eta
=1+{5\sqrt3\over19}
=1.455802844097073\ldots,
\]

and (RB.26)-(RB.27) give

\[
C_\eta=0.764079936006341\ldots,
\qquad
\Theta_\eta=0.701182390406373\ldots>{2\pi\over9}.
\]

Therefore one exterior isolate can witness at most

\[
\boxed{8}
\tag{RB.33}
\]

of these apices.

Let `U` be the number of distinct `1/19`-shallow apices with an upper blocker, and let
`Z` be the number of distinct exterior isolates witnessing the remaining shallow
apices. Equations (RB.8) and (RB.33) give

\[
8Z\ge N_{sh}(1/19)-U.
\tag{RB.34}
\]

Therefore either `U>=N_sh/9` or `Z>=N_sh/9`. Using (RB.32),

\[
\boxed{
\max\{U,Z\}
\ge0.0423993163189017975\ldots D-o(D).
}
\tag{RB.35}
\]

The two branches are quantitative:

1. **normal-aligned near-diameter branch.** At least the amount in (RB.35) of
   distinct shallow apices have a source point `y` with
   \[
   |x-y|>D-{5\sqrt3\over19}
   =D-0.455802844097073\ldots,
   \]
   and, in base coordinates,
   \[
   {|p|\over q}< {5\sqrt3\over37}
   =0.234060919941740\ldots;
   \]
2. **exterior-isolate branch.** At least the amount in (RB.35) of distinct actual
   contact-isolated source points lie beyond the corresponding loaded support lines by
   more than
   \[
   Y_-(\alpha_\eta)
   ={8\sqrt3\over19}
   =0.729284550555317\ldots.
   \]

An isolate created as an exact new reflected contact has the stronger depth

\[
Y_+(\alpha_\eta)
={21\sqrt3\over38}
=0.957185972603853\ldots.
\tag{RB.36}
\]

## 7. Frontier effect

The current tooth branch no longer stops at

```text
many shallow teeth -> many unweighted inward contacts.
```

It now has the stronger source-level reduction

```text
many shallow teeth
 -> pairwise distinct shallow apices
 -> reflect each apex across its loaded support line
 -> either a normal-aligned point lies within O(1) of diameter from that apex
    or an actual exterior isolate blocks/pays for the move
 -> angular packing bounds exterior-isolate reuse
 -> linear near-diameter-braced family or linear exterior-isolate family.
```

This still does not prove a contradiction. The near-diameter pairs in the first
branch need not be exact diameter edges, and a linear exterior-isolate population in
the second branch is compatible with the canonical `o(D^2)` full-core alternative.
Those are the two precise remaining interfaces exposed by this reduction.

The next high-value theorem should couple the first branch to the dense convex
actual-diameter thrackle, or combine the second branch with exterior-cap packing and
contact-minimal exchange. Either route now starts from a linear family with explicit
geometry rather than from stress weights.

## 8. Reproducibility

The arithmetic constants and the finite geometric inequalities are checked by

```bash
python3 research/verify_target_c_shallow_tooth_reflection_blockers.py
```

The verifier is a regression certificate, not a substitute for the proofs above.
